# Family A35: the RabbitMQ protocol (internal/rabbit/protocol).
# See hack/bakeoff.sh.
PKG=./internal/rabbit/protocol
BENCH=BenchmarkRabbitProtocol
REF=amqp091
AUTO_KEY=rabbitprotocol
KIND_DIM=""
# ADR-013 collects the §9 rows that need a broker and are therefore decided in
# Part B.
ADR=13
TITLE="RabbitMQ protocol: AMQP 0-9-1 against the AMQP 1.0 profile for RabbitMQ >= 4 (A35)"
# This family's arms ARE the two drivers -- drivers/rabbitmq over amqp091-go
# and drivers/rabbitmq/amqp10 over drivers/amqp10 -- so no package belonging to
# either of them could hold both, and a root-module `go test` cannot reach a
# nested driver module anyway. What lives here is the part
# of each protocol that is a DECISION rather than a client library: the
# supported-version floor, the §4.8 feature subset, the address form, and the
# property mapping both drivers run on every publication and every delivery.

#
# CRITERION is the benchmark configuration's decision column for this row, quoted.
# MATRIX is the IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX grid the campaign measures the family at.
# DECIDED_AT names the benchmark-name segments the criterion is read at, and
# DECIDED_ON the metrics; `make bench-lint` lists the family's benchmarks at
# the declared grid and fails when a term or a metric is missing from the rows
# every kind has in common, which is the only data `bakeoff decide` reads.
CRITERION="supported-version compatibility, feature subset, perf -> 0-9-1 default; 1.0 conditional profile"
# MATRIX=default is the only grid: internal/rabbit/protocol has no
# IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX branch. The dimensions are the mapping's two directions and
# §10.2's own header count, which is what the property mapping's cost varies
# with.
MATRIX=default
DECIDED_AT="carry restore headers=0 headers=4"
# TWO of the criterion's three terms are facts rather than measurements, and
# both are reported as metrics so that the scorecard carries the whole
# criterion rather than the third of it that has a clock attached:
#
#   min-broker  supported-version compatibility, as the major version the
#               protocol needs. The AMQP 1.0 profile refuses anything below 4,
#               which is what settles the default before any measurement.
#   features    the feature subset, as the number of the §4.8 inventory the
#               protocol supports NATIVELY. It is computed from
#               Protocol.Support rather than measured, and the conformance
#               suites are what prove the table in both directions.
#
# ns/op and allocs/op are the third term, for the part of it that is honestly
# measurable with no broker: the property mapping each protocol's message model
# demands, on the hot path of every publication. The arms really do differ
# there -- AMQP 0-9-1's per-message TTL is a decimal string of milliseconds and
# AMQP 1.0's is a duration, so one arm allocates per publication with a TTL and
# the other does not -- and TestAllocs pins that in both directions.
DECIDED_ON="min-broker features ns/op allocs/op"
# What is not measured here is the two protocols' throughput against a real
# broker, which needs a broker and two client libraries.
CRITERION_GAP="throughput against a real broker=needs a broker and two client libraries -- ./conformance/suites runs the same §11.2 suite through both drivers against the pinned image (make integration DRIVER=rabbitmq) and ./conformance/matrix exercises the version floor (make matrix DRIVER=rabbitmq)"
# The family's decision rule is `rules["A35"]` in
# benchmarks/cmd/bakeoff/rules.go. Both arms SHIP -- §7.2 asks for both, and
# says the 1.0 profile is "never silently substituted" -- so the "winner" is
# which protocol a caller gets by not choosing, not which one is deleted. The
# rule fixes it at amqp091 because that is the only arm every broker in the
# version matrix speaks, and records the 1.0 profile as a module a caller
# imports deliberately.
